\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

The optimization of Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) streaming has evolved from rigid heuristics to sophisticated learning-based control systems. The existing literature can be broadly categorized into three paradigms: fixed-rule heuristics, pure reinforcement learning, and infrastructure-assisted approaches.

\subsection{Heuristics and Control-Theoretic Methods}
Early ABR algorithms relied on fixed control laws derived from observable metrics. \textit{Buffer-Based Algorithms (BBA)} \cite{huang2014buffer} prioritize buffer occupancy, selecting bitrates to maintain the reservoir within a pre-defined safety range. While robust to throughput estimation errors, BBA often exhibits slow convergence during bandwidth ramp-ups and induces unnecessary switching when the buffer is near saturation.
To address this, \textit{Control-Theoretic methods} such as MPC \cite{yin2015control} and hybrid models \cite{liu2023hybrid} frame ABR as a resource allocation problem over a sliding horizon. Sobhani et al. \cite{sobhani2015video} proposed a fuzzy logic-based controller to handle bandwidth uncertainties proactively. However, these model-based approaches rely heavily on accurate throughput predictors. In modern stochastic networks (e.g., 5G mmWave), the "model mismatch" between idealized equations and real-world dynamics often results in suboptimal performance.

\subsection{Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) in ABR}
The advent of DRL shifted the focus from "modeling" to "learning". Gadaleta et al. introduced \textit{D-DASH} \cite{gadaleta2017ddash}, a framework utilizing Deep Q-Networks (DQN) to map state features to quality decisions. Similarly, the seminal work \textit{Pensieve} \cite{mao2017neural} employed an A3C (Asynchronous Advantage Actor-Critic) agent to learn policies directly from raw experience, demonstrating superior performance over MPC. Subsequent works like Fugu \cite{yan2019learning} further refined learning environments using real-world deployments.
Despite their success, these pure DRL approaches suffer from the \textit{"Black Box" challenge}. They lack inherent safety guarantees; a reward-driven agent might learn aggressive policies that maximize average bitrate but risk catastrophic buffer depletion during rare "tail" events. Furthermore, standard agents are "future-blind," reacting only to past observations without awareness of upcoming chunk size variations.

\subsection{Edge-Assisted and Network-Aware Streaming}
Recognizing the limitations of client-only adaptation, recent works have explored shifting complexity to the network edge. Mehrabi et al. \cite{mehrabi2019edge} proposed an Edge Computing Assisted adaptation scheme, utilizing MEC (Multi-Access Edge Computing) servers to perform throughput prediction and bitrate selection on behalf of mobile clients. Similarly, Kim and Chung \cite{kim2023http} and Kang and Chung \cite{kang2023adaptive} leveraged edge computing to improve fairness among multiple competing clients using RL-based schedulers.
While edge-assisted solutions offer global optimization capabilities, they require significant infrastructure upgrades and server-side deployment. In contrast, our work focuses on empowering the \textit{client-side} logic. We argue that a robust client should not depend on external infrastructure assistance. By integrating \textit{Lyapunov optimization}, we achieve stability comparable to edge-assisted methods, but within a purely client-centric, deployable framework.

\subsection{Safe Reinforcement Learning}
To address the instability of pure RL without relying on edge infrastructure, the concept of "Safe RL" has gained significant traction \cite{wang2025safe}. This involves constraining the agent's exploration to avoid dangerous states (e.g., empty buffer). Recent studies \cite{tian2022towards, xiao2025qoe} have explored injecting safety constraints into the learning loop. Our work aligns with this emerging trend but introduces a novel mechanism: instead of simple action masking, we inject a \textit{Lyapunov-guided virtual queue constraint} directly into the PPO reward function. Combined with our explicit \textit{Future-Awareness} (feeding next-chunk sizes), this hybrid design bridges the gap between the adaptability of DRL and the mathematical robustness of control theory.

