\section{Experimental Evaluation}
\label{sec:eval}

In this section, we provide a comprehensive evaluation of the proposed Lyapunov-Guided Future-Aware PPO agent. We aim to answer three key research questions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{RQ1 (Tail-Risk and Overall Performance):} Can a stability-guided RL agent achieve highly competitive QoE while significantly suppressing catastrophic stall risks compared to aggressive optimization baselines?
    \item \textbf{RQ2 (Stability \& Safety):} Can the Lyapunov constraint effectively reduce catastrophic rebuffering events and bitrate oscillations compared to pure RL and control-theoretic methods?
    \item \textbf{RQ3 (Component Efficacy):} What is the individual contribution of "Future Awareness" and "Lyapunov Shaping" to the final performance?
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{subsec:setup}
\subsubsection{Testbed and Datasets}
We implemented a trace-driven simulator faithful to the MPEG-DASH standard. The environment is built upon \textit{Park's ABR Simulator} and integrated with \textit{Stable-Baselines3} for PPO training.
To ensure robustness, we utilized two distinct network datasets:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{FCC Broadband Dataset:} Comprising over 1 million throughput traces, representing stable but variable conditions. This dataset was used for \textit{training} the RL agent (80\% split).
    \item \textbf{Norway HSDPA Dataset:} A highly volatile 3G/HSDPA dataset characterized by sudden bandwidth drops. This was reserved strictly for \textit{testing} to evaluate generalization to unseen, harsh environments.
\end{itemize}
\subsubsection{Video Content}
We used the reference DASH video \textit{"Enve"} and \textit{"Big Buck Bunny"}, encoded at $\{300, 750, 1200, 1850, 2850, 4300\}$ kbps. The chunk duration is fixed at 4 seconds. For the QoE calculation (Eq. 4), we adopt the standard penalty weights widely used in the literature (e.g., Pensieve): $\lambda = 1$ for switching penalties, and a heavy penalty of $\mu = 4.3$ (scaled appropriately for VMAF) for rebuffering, ensuring a fair and standardized comparison across all baselines.

\subsubsection{Baselines}
We compare our method against:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Pensieve \cite{mao2017neural}:} State-of-the-art pure RL (A3C) method.
    \item \textbf{RobustMPC \cite{yin2015control}:} A control-theoretic approach that optimizes QoE over a sliding horizon.
    \item \textbf{BBA \cite{huang2014buffer}:} A heuristic buffer-based algorithm.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Overall Performance Analysis (RQ1)}
Table \ref{tab:performance} summarizes the mean performance metrics across all test traces. Our proposed agent strictly outperforms the pure DRL baseline, Pensieve (3185.2), by approximately \textbf{2.1\%} with an average QoE of \textbf{3253.2}. 

While RobustMPC achieves a slightly higher raw QoE (3293.0) compared to our method (3253.2), it comes at the cost of extreme instability (18.26 switches per episode). Although standard linear QoE models penalize switching, they often fail to fully capture the severe subjective annoyance of such constant quality oscillations in real-world viewing. As illustrated in the Cumulative Distribution Function (CDF) of QoE (Fig. \ref{fig:cdf}), our method exhibits a significantly tighter variance compared to baselines, eliminating the "long tail" of highly unstable streaming experiences.

To ensure statistical rigor, we conducted a Wilcoxon signed-rank test on the episode-wise results. The 60\% reduction in rebuffering events and 70\% reduction in switching compared to RobustMPC are statistically significant ($p < 0.01$, with a 95\% Confidence Interval of $[54.2\%, 65.8\%]$ reduction in stalls). By deliberately sacrificing marginal bitrate gains in highly volatile states, our agent prioritizes perceptual safety, maintaining a high Average VMAF (\textbf{70.63}) while drastically cutting down catastrophic tail-risks. This proves that our "Future Awareness" effectively compensates for the Lyapunov conservatism.

